---
source: tests/steps/l01_05_lexer_strings/strings.rs
expression: "out.join(\"\\n\")"
---
"\"unclosed"
    [0..9] this string is not closed before the end of the file. Write the closing `"` where its text ends.
"\"two\nlines\""
    [0..4] this `"` string reaches the end of its line without its closing `"`, and a `"` string ends on its line. Close it on this line, or write a string over several lines as `"""`.
"\"\"\"never closed\n"
    [0..16] this string is not closed before the end of the file. Write the closing `"""` where its text ends.
"\"\\q is not an escape\""
    [1..3] `\q` is no escape. The escapes are `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\0`, `\\`, `\"`, `\$` and `\u{…}`. Write `\\` for a backslash itself.
"\"\\u{110000}\""
    [1..11] `\u{110000}` names no Unicode character: a code point is at most 10FFFF and is not a surrogate, D800 to DFFF. Write the code point of the character meant.
"\"\\u{D800}\""
    [1..9] `\u{D800}` names no Unicode character: a code point is at most 10FFFF and is not a surrogate, D800 to DFFF. Write the code point of the character meant.
"\"\\u0041\""
    [1..3] `\u` starts a code point, written as hexadecimal digits in braces, as `\u{1F600}`, and no braced digits follow it. Write the code point in braces, or `\\u` for a backslash followed by `u`.
"\"\\u{}\""
    [1..3] `\u` starts a code point, written as hexadecimal digits in braces, as `\u{1F600}`, and no braced digits follow it. Write the code point in braces, or `\\u` for a backslash followed by `u`.
"\"\"\"\n    in\n  out\n    \"\"\""
    [11..13] this line of a `"""` string is indented less than the closing `"""`, whose indentation is removed from every line of the string. Indent this line at least as far as the closing `"""`, or move the closing `"""` left.
"\"\"\"\n    in\n\tout\n    \"\"\""
    [11..12] this line of a `"""` string is indented less than the closing `"""`, whose indentation is removed from every line of the string. Indent this line at least as far as the closing `"""`, or move the closing `"""` left.
"\"ends in a backslash\\"
    [20..21] the file ends after a `\`, which starts an escape in a string. Write the escape, or `\\` for a backslash itself, and close the string.
